{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "The current mapping gives a single smooth dependence of q_star on mutation pressure and selection, with heterozygote cost fixed by a constant dominance coefficient h.",
      "child_relation": "Replace constant dominance by frequency-dependent effective dominance caused by inbreeding/selfing, so the same mu and s produce a steeper low-q decline and altered curvature because homozygotes are exposed above Hardy-Weinberg expectations.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "mu",
        "s",
        "h",
        "nu"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "When varying mu at fixed s,h,nu, the equilibrium rises more slowly than the parent predicts in regimes where extra homozygosity matters; equivalently, at fixed mutation pressure, increasing effective homozygote exposure lowers q_star beyond what any single constant-h parent fit can match across weak- and strong-mutation conditions."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "mutation_load_ratio",
        "expression": "mu/s",
        "role": "Forward-mutation pressure relative to viability removal"
      },
      {
        "name": "reversibility_ratio",
        "expression": "nu/mu",
        "role": "Importance of back mutation relative to forward input"
      },
      {
        "name": "heterozygote_scale",
        "expression": "h",
        "role": "Baseline direct selection against rare deleterious copies"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Most visible when the deleterious allele is rare to moderate and homozygote excess is biologically plausible, so recessive exposure rather than heterozygote cost controls much of the balance; weakest when mating is effectively random and Hardy-Weinberg proportions hold closely.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Within training, variation in mu and s already probes low- versus higher-q regimes; the refined mechanism changes curvature across those regimes rather than only rescaling one parameter, and this contrast extends to holdout combinations with the same mutation-selection scales.",
    "continuation_path": "In the random-mating limit with no excess homozygosity, the child reduces to the parent law exactly.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The parent assumes genotype frequencies are always at Hardy-Weinberg proportions, so recessive alleles are exposed to selection only through q^2 homozygotes implied by random mating.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The population is effectively infinite, randomly mating, and at deterministic equilibrium.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Mild inbreeding or selfing increases homozygote formation, exposing recessive deleterious alleles to viability selection more often than under random mating and thereby altering deterministic mutation-selection balance.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "mu",
      "s",
      "h",
      "nu"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the random-mating part of the equilibrium assumption, not to add a new unrelated process. The parent's target mapping has a fixed-environment shortcut: for given mu, s, h, and nu it treats genotype exposure as if Hardy-Weinberg always holds. Under partial dominance plus back mutation, the parent already captures direct heterozygote selection and reversibility, but it still forces one specific curvature of q_star versus mutation pressure because homozygote exposure scales only through the random-mating q^2 term. If mating departs from random, recessive copies are unmasked more efficiently, especially in the low-q regime where homozygote excess can compete with the small heterozygote term. Vary mu while holding s, h, and nu fixed: the parent predicts one smooth rise governed by constant h and random-mating homozygote production, whereas the child would show stronger suppression of q_star at low to intermediate q and thus a changed curvature. This is a changed target mapping, not merely an auxiliary trajectory change, because the equilibrium q_star itself shifts for the same supplied inputs. The effect is scientifically admissible when there is non-negligible excess homozygosity; it disappears under random mating, which provides the parent-recovery limit. A single refit of existing parent parameters cannot generally absorb this across conditions, because changing h only alters direct selection on heterozygotes and cannot reproduce the distinct q-dependent increase in recessive exposure caused by homozygote excess over both weak- and stronger-mutation regimes."
  },
